  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "section": "narrative",
      "text": "Halifax DT696 of 158 Squadron took off from Rufforth at 18.16 on the evening of 14 February 1943, bound for Cologne. One port engine failed soon after take-off. The bombs were jettisoned near Appleton Roebuck, but the heavily loaded aircraft stalled and crashed at 18.30 just west of Stillingfleet. All seven crew were killed.",
      "cites": [
        "C1",
        "C6",
        "C7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "section": "memorial_details",
      "text": "A brass plaque on a wooden backboard hangs in St Helen's church, Stillingfleet. It was dedicated on 14 February 2008 and unveiled by H N Mottershead, President of the 158 Squadron Association. It gives Flood's rank as P/O, repeating a CWGC error. A second memorial stone placed near the crash site was mistakenly moved to a nearby farm, according to Yorkshire Aircraft.",
      "cites": [
        "C9",
        "C14",
        "C15",
        "C17"
      ]
    },
    {
      "section": "community_connection",
      "text": "Local resident Yvonne Clarke arranged the plaque, helped by aviation historians David Thompson and the late Eddie Fell, who traced the crew's families. IWM lists the Stillingfleet Chroniclers as sponsor.",
      "cites": [
        "C13",
        "C16"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "custodian_status": "unknown-confirmed",
  "retractions": [
    {
      "field": "community_connection",
      "reason": "Existing text is only the plaque's closing line, 'Their Names Liveth For Evermore', which is not community connection."
    }
  ],
  "notes": "CUSTODIAN: not found. The plaque is inside St Helen's church (Church of England), Stillingfleet, which implies the church looks after it, but no source says so, so I have not claimed it. IWM sponsor: 'Stillingfleet Chroniclers' (probably a local history group, unverified, no site found). Yvonne Clarke arranged the plaque in 2008 (Yorkshire Aircraft); she is a named private individual, no contact given. Next step: ask the church/PCC or the Stillingfleet Chroniclers. DISCREPANCY: Yorkshire Aircraft says the aircraft took off at 18.16 and crashed 18.30 on the evening of 14 Feb; the plaque says 'Sunday 14th February 1943' (fits). IBCC mentions the plaque too (spelling 'Sillingflet'), not fetched. Our record's lat/long put the stone at the church; second stone said to be at a nearby farm. Crew nationalities other than Cains are inferred in the source, not claimed. IWM photo by David Lyons exists but I did not add it (no capture in research/iwm; credit/URL taken from our record only)."
}